{{!--
	Blitzy table of contents: one DOM instance, two presentations, rendered by
	self-recursion.

	Invoked from skin.mustache and, for every nesting level below the first, from
	itself. Self-recursion is sanctioned rather than tolerated: SkinMustache's
	template parser calls enableRecursivePartials( true ) and the comment on that
	call in includes/Skin/SkinMustache.php reads "For table of contents
	rendering." Blitzy ships exactly nine templates (rule R12), so there is no
	TableOfContents__list / __line pair to delegate to the way Vector has.

	================================================================
	INVOCATION CONTRACT -- this partial must be entered with the
	`data-toc` object as the context, never with the top-level
	template data:

	    {{#is-blitzy-toc-available}}
	        {{#data-toc}}{{>TableOfContents}}{{/data-toc}}
	    {{/is-blitzy-toc-available}}

	`array-sections` lives inside `data-toc`, so entering at the top level would
	render an empty list. Worse, the leaf branch below keys on `array-sections`
	being empty, and at the top level that key is absent rather than empty, so a
	stray empty <li> would be emitted -- an anchor with no accessible name, which
	is a blocking rule R10 failure. This was measured, not assumed: a top-level
	invocation produces one more <li> than there are sections. No Mustache
	construct that is safe to use here (see DISPATCH) can tell a deep leaf section
	apart from the top-level context, so this is a genuine contract and not a
	preference.

	`is-blitzy-toc-available` comes from BlitzyViewModel and is true only when
	`data-toc` reports at least one section, so this partial is never entered for a
	page with no headings or for one carrying __NOTOC__.
	================================================================

	DISPATCH -- three mutually exclusive branches, none of which pushes a scalar
	context. Two obvious discriminators are both wrong, and both failure modes are
	silent, so they are recorded here to stop a future simplification from
	reintroducing them.

	1. NOT `number-section-count`. It looks ideal because core sets it on the
	   `data-toc` root and never on a section. But MediaWiki compiles templates
	   with LightnCandy's FLAG_MUSTACHELOOKUP, which resolves a missing key by
	   walking UP the context stack, so from inside `array-sections` the parent
	   `data-toc` is still visible and the key still resolves. Measured: it
	   returns the root's count from inside a section. Dispatching on it repeats
	   the whole card -- nav, checkbox and label -- at every nesting level, which
	   duplicates three fixed ids per level.

	2. NOT `{{#anchor}}` for the section branch, even though `{{^anchor}}` is
	   correct for the root branch. `anchor` holds a STRING, and a Mustache
	   section over a truthy scalar makes that scalar the new context. Inside a
	   runtime partial the walk-up out of a scalar context skips the section hash
	   it came from and lands on the root, so every sibling key -- `anchor`,
	   `toclevel`, `number`, `linkAnchor`, `line` -- resolves to nothing.
	   Measured: every entry renders as
	       <li id="blitzy-toc-" class="blitzy-toc__item blitzy-toc__item--level-">
	   with an empty number, empty text and href="#". Inverted sections and
	   sections over booleans do not push a context, so all three branches below
	   are built from those two forms only. For the same reason {{../key}} is not
	   an escape route: MediaWiki does not enable LightnCandy's FLAG_PARENT and
	   compilation fails outright if it is used.

	   {{^anchor}}            root      `anchor` is absent from `data-toc` and
	                                    from the top-level data, and present on
	                                    every section.
	   {{#is-parent-section}} branch    a section that has children.
	   {{^array-sections}}    leaf      a section that has none.

	   Exhaustive and exclusive, because core derives the flag as
	   'is-parent-section' => $childSections !== [], making it the exact
	   complement of `array-sections` being empty. The leaf branch keys on
	   `array-sections` rather than on {{^is-parent-section}} deliberately: the two
	   are equivalent on a section, but at the `data-toc` context
	   `is-parent-section` is absent and therefore falsy, which would emit a stray
	   <li> beside the card, whereas `array-sections` is non-empty there and
	   correctly suppresses the branch.

	   The <li> and its link are spelled out in both section branches. That
	   repetition is forced: no key is truthy on every section AND safe to open a
	   section over, so there is no single guard the shared markup could sit under.

	DATA CONSUMED -- every key below is supplied by core, none is invented.
	`data-toc` is built by Skin\Components\SkinComponentTableOfContents; each
	section is Parsoid's SectionMetadata::toLegacy() merged with the two flags.

	    array-sections       nested child sections; always present on a section,
	                         empty on a leaf.
	    is-parent-section    true when the section has children.
	    anchor               heading id fragment. Used ONLY to derive our ids.
	    linkAnchor           heading link fragment. Used ONLY for href.
	    toclevel             nesting depth, 1-based. Drives the level modifier.
	    number               section number, e.g. "2.1".
	    line                 heading text as RAW parser HTML.
	    msg-toc              core message `toc`, "Contents". Escaped text, because
	                         SkinMustache builds msg- keys with ->text().
	    msg-blitzy-toc-toggle  accessible name of the disclosure. Requires
	                         `blitzy-toc-toggle` in skin.json's `messages` list,
	                         or it renders empty and the toggle loses its name.

	ESCAPING -- `line` is the only value emitted raw, and it is raw because it is
	parser output that legitimately carries inline formatting such as <i> and <b>;
	rule R2 requires it to pass through untouched, and escaping it would print the
	tags as visible text. Everything else uses {{ }}. `anchor` and `linkAnchor` are
	attribute values, not markup, so they are escaped even though core derives them
	from the same headings.

	Why it is safe to place `line` INSIDE the entry's <a>, even though nesting one
	anchor in another is invalid HTML: core strips anchors out of `line` before it
	reaches a skin. Measured against MediaWiki 1.43.9 -- "== See [[Main Page]] for
	more ==" arrives as "See Main Page for more" and
	"== External [https://example.org site] link ==" as "External site link", while
	"== Mixed ''italic'' and [[Photosynthesis|linked]] ==" arrives as
	"Mixed <i>italic</i> and linked". Inline formatting survives; links do not.
	Vector relies on exactly the same guarantee in production. So do not "defend"
	against a link here: stripping or re-escaping `line` would break rule R2 and
	would corrupt the italics that legitimately do arrive.

	IDS -- `blitzy-toc-checkbox` and `blitzy-toc-button` are fixed literals that
	TableOfContents.less and resources/skins.blitzy.js/tableOfContents.js key
	against; per-entry ids are `blitzy-toc-<anchor>` and, for a sublist,
	`blitzy-toc-<anchor>-sublist`. Every id is either a literal or a pure function
	of core-supplied data, so two renders of one revision are identical, which is
	what rule R9's byte-identical captures need. Nothing here is generated,
	random or time-derived.

	DELIBERATELY ABSENT
	  * Any <h1>-<h6>. The page carries exactly one <h1>, core's #firstHeading;
	    a heading here would break heading order for no gain (rule R10). The
	    card's visible title is a <span> and its accessible name is the <nav>
	    landmark's aria-label.
	  * Any active or current class. There is no server-side notion of the
	    section being read, so inventing one would make the markup vary between
	    identical requests and break rule R9. tableOfContents.js adds the active
	    class at runtime and TableOfContents.less draws the purple rule.
	  * Any width test. The sticky card at 1024px and up and the inline
	    disclosure below it are the SAME markup styled two ways, so the checkbox,
	    the label and the target are unconditional. Emitting the list twice would
	    duplicate every id on the page, and omitting either presentation would
	    make a rule R11 assertion fail against a missing element rather than
	    report a difference.
	  * Any style attribute, colour or dimension. Rule R3 keeps every value in
	    tokens.less, reached through TableOfContents.less.
	  * Any directional attribute. The card's inherently directional details --
	    the active section's leading border, the indentation of nested levels --
	    are the stylesheet's to author flip-aware under rule R6.
	  * aria-haspopup. Core documents it as optional and specifically for popup
	    and menu elements; this is a disclosure, so aria-expanded alone is the
	    accurate promise.

	The disclosure is core's documented checkbox hack: a visually hidden checkbox
	followed by its label and then its target, all siblings, with `aria-expanded`
	on the CHECKBOX rather than the label because core's updateAriaExpanded()
	deprecated the label form in 1.38. Core binds nothing by these class names, so
	the :checked sibling rule in TableOfContents.less is the entire mechanism and it
	keeps working with scripting disabled, as rule R4 requires.

	`role="button"` is part of that documented contract, and core is explicit that
	the attributes and the script together are what "give the checkbox the
	behavior and semantics of a button". Two consequences belong to the script
	rather than to this file, and both were confirmed by measurement with scripting
	switched off: `aria-expanded` stays "false" while the list is open, and Enter
	does not activate the control, because a native checkbox toggles on Space
	alone. Opening and closing still work unaided, which is all rule R4 requires;
	tableOfContents.js owns keeping aria-expanded in step and, if it is wanted,
	Enter activation. The checkbox must therefore stay merely visually hidden --
	never `display: none`, never `hidden`, never a negative tabindex -- or it
	leaves the focus order and the disclosure stops being keyboard operable.
--}}
{{^anchor}}
<nav class="blitzy-toc" aria-label="{{msg-toc}}">
	<span class="blitzy-toc__title">{{msg-toc}}</span>
	<input type="checkbox" id="blitzy-toc-checkbox" class="blitzy-toc__checkbox mw-checkbox-hack-checkbox" role="button" aria-labelledby="blitzy-toc-button" aria-expanded="false">
	<label id="blitzy-toc-button" class="blitzy-toc__toggle mw-checkbox-hack-button" for="blitzy-toc-checkbox" aria-hidden="true">{{msg-blitzy-toc-toggle}}</label>
	<div class="blitzy-toc__content mw-checkbox-hack-target">
		<ul class="blitzy-toc__list">
			{{#array-sections}}{{>TableOfContents}}{{/array-sections}}
		</ul>
	</div>
</nav>
{{/anchor}}
{{#is-parent-section}}
<li id="blitzy-toc-{{anchor}}" class="blitzy-toc__item blitzy-toc__item--level-{{toclevel}}">
	<a class="blitzy-toc__link" href="#{{linkAnchor}}"><span class="blitzy-toc__number">{{number}}</span> <span class="blitzy-toc__text">{{{line}}}</span></a>
	<ul id="blitzy-toc-{{anchor}}-sublist" class="blitzy-toc__list">
		{{#array-sections}}{{>TableOfContents}}{{/array-sections}}
	</ul>
</li>
{{/is-parent-section}}
{{^array-sections}}
<li id="blitzy-toc-{{anchor}}" class="blitzy-toc__item blitzy-toc__item--level-{{toclevel}}">
	<a class="blitzy-toc__link" href="#{{linkAnchor}}"><span class="blitzy-toc__number">{{number}}</span> <span class="blitzy-toc__text">{{{line}}}</span></a>
</li>
{{/array-sections}}
